              "start": 131,
              "end": 134
            }
          ],
          "path": [],
          "title": "Subtracting from a Nat can go below zero",
          "why": "Nat has no negative values, so a subtraction result is typed Int. The checker cannot assume a guard keeps it at zero or above.",
          "nextStep": "If the value can go below zero, change Nat to Int in its declaration; for machine state keep the same finite range. Keep Nat only where the value can never decrease.",
          "location": {
            "file": "counter.pym",
            "start": 144,
            "end": 149,
            "line": 2,
            "column": 35,
            "excerpt": "flow next(n: Nat) -> Nat { return n - 1; }"
          },
